# gating — an IN-PROCESS, before-veto PORT for deciding allow/deny on an inbound
# dispatch, declared once and used by every domain's hexagon (the bind
# `Aggregate.gated_by("authorize")` references this family by its how-verb). The
# shared vocabulary lives here ; a domain never redeclares it.
#
# Two-color rule, the IN-PROCESS pole : where payment / authentication are EFFECT
# families (out-of-process, the adapter host runs them async), a gate is a REPLY
# family — and REPLY ALREADY MEANS in-process / DI'd / called inline (the same
# property that makes persisted_by inline). A gate MUST be in-process : it vetoes
# BEFORE the act and returns a verdict the dispatch waits on, so it can never
# await an async edge without breaking the two-color rule. So `in_process` is NOT
# a new marker — it is DERIVED from `signal :reply`. (Phase 6 A3/A4 sketched an
# explicit in_process marker ; the reply signal already carries it, so deriving
# it keeps the family vocabulary minimal — no keyword to invent.)
#
# The gate is the inverse of a Driver (driving.bluebook) : a Driver names its own
# inbound SCHEDULE and runs OUT-of-process ; a Gate names its own inbound CHECK
# and runs IN-process, synchronous, blocking the dispatch it gates.
#   Driver : Gate  ::  out-of-process clock : in-process check.
#
# The how-verb `gated_by` takes the CHECK as its argument, exactly as
# `persisted_by` takes the adapter : `Aggregate.gated_by("authorize")`,
# `gated_by("authenticate")`, or `gated_by("SomeAggregate.some_query")` (a bluebook
# query run read-only with the caller's principal as the reserved `actor` kwarg,
# allowing iff it returns a row). ONE verb covers authn and authz alike — the
# difference is the check name + the order (authn orders before authz), never a
# second verb. (Phase 6 sketched `authenticated_by` as a sibling verb ; it is
# redundant — `gated_by("authenticate")` already says it, check-as-argument.)
#
# A gate carries NO per-deployment config, so this family declares NO fields :
# its phase / check / pattern / order are DOMAIN attributes on Storehouse::Gate,
# not .world values (like the Memory adapter, which carries no .world block). And
# no `produces` : a reply family returns its verdict, it emits no produced fact
# (unlike payment's :payment_ref or authentication's :verification_ref).
#
# CONCEIVED HEXAGON SURFACE (parser-pending — the production that reads it is the
# kernel/parity sibling card, NOT wired here, exactly as driving's `interval`
# sugar is conceived-but-unwired) :
#
#   # cross-cutting standing gates (pattern "*"), the mirror of `driving on cron` :
#   gating on dispatch do
#     gate "authenticate", phase: before, order: 10, pattern: "*"
#     gate "authorize",    phase: before, order: 20, pattern: "*"
#   end
#
#   # scoped sugar — one aggregate's dispatches (pattern derived from the FQN) :
#   Pizzas::Order.gated_by("SomeAggregate.member_of_owning_org")
#
# Today gates are authored through the storehouse door via Storehouse::Gate.Declare
# (no DSL) ; the runtime's hydrate_middleware already PROJECTS the active Gate
# records into the before/after middleware stack. Phase 6 Half B inverts that flow
# — the hexagon surface above becomes the SOURCE and Gate.Declare its projection,
# the way the Procfile is the projection of declared Drivers — keeping Gate.Declare
# + the self-seeded standing `authorize` working untouched until the projection lands.
Hecks.family "gating" do
  verb   "gated_by"   # the how-verb the bind hangs off the aggregate FQN ; the CHECK is its argument
  signal :reply       # returns an allow/deny verdict INLINE — and reply => in-process (the gate vetoes before the act)
end
